  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1205 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nSaturday begins the long term period with a shortwave trough \nclipping southeastern CO and northeastern NM late day. With a strong \nattendant 100-120kt H3 jetmax rounding the trough axis, aided by a \n994mb MSLP lee side surface low near CO Springs, strong westerly to \nnorthwesterly winds fill in across much of northern and central NM \nSaturday afternoon. The strongest sustained winds of 20-30kts \ngusting 40-50kts will focus over the high terrain along the \nContinental Divide and along and immediately east of the central \nmountain chain and central highlands. Forecast confidence is \nmoderate for those strongest winds reaching 50kts near the central \nhighlands as cross-sectional analysis of numerical model output \nshows these strongest wind speeds just above ground level. The \nquestion remains how amplified will the mountain wave activity \ncoupled with how deep vertical mixing will be to work these stronger \nwind speed gusts down the surface. A slate of Wind Advisories is now \nlooking likely for the Saturday afternoon period, at minimum \ncovering these wind prone areas of the highlands immediately east of \nthe central mountain chain. Hazardous crosswinds on area highways \nwith strong headwinds along the I-40 climb between Santa Rosa to \nClines Corners will be the main impacts from this. Another side \neffect to all of this will be the warmest high temperatures of the \nforecast period through eastern NM from downsloping. Highs climbing \ninto the low to mid 70s will be the rule there Saturday afternoon. \n\nA secondary weather hazard will be minor winter precipitation \nclipping the northern Sangre de Cristo's and adjacent Raton Pass and \nJohnson/Bartlett Mesas area Saturday night into Sunday morning. \nThereafter, a quick and potent cold front backs southward thru \neastern NM Sunday dropping temperatures 10F-25F back into the 40s \nand 50s. This temperature drop will be noticeable but less potent \nfurther west into the Rio Grande Valley. \n\nThe temperature roller coaster continues Monday with a return of \nbreezy to windy westerlies returning and bringing another round of \ndownslope warming through eastern NM. Highs there climb right back \ninto the 60s Monday afternoon. This above normal warmth remains \nareawide Tuesday with a shortwave ridge of high pressure expected to \nslide west to east across the region. Thereafter, the extended \nperiod continues to advertise a more active weather pattern bringing \na Pacific storm system into the western CONUS late next week. \nCurrent model trends favor this system opening and weakening as it \nreaches NM with light to moderate precipitation favoring west facing \nslopes of the high terrain at minimum.",
